+
+## Layout
+
+${options.bare ? '' : '- `specs/main.spec.md` — the starter spec\n'}- \`specs/\` — specs (source of truth)
+- \`src/\` — generated code
+- \`tests/\`
+- \`.speclangrc\` — project configuration
+- \`scripts/speclang.cjs\` — local CLI shim (see below)
+
+## Commands
+
+\`\`\`bash
+npm run validate    # validate every spec under specs/
+npm run generate    # generate code from specs
+npm run start       # start the SpecLang daemon
+npm run speclang -- <command> [args...]   # any other CLI command
+
+node scripts/speclang.cjs --version      # same thing without npm
+\`\`\`
+
+\`npm install\` is optional: this project has no third-party dependencies. The
+npm scripts never call a bare \`speclang\`, so nothing has to be on \`PATH\`.
+
+## Which SpecLang CLI is used
+
+\`scripts/speclang.cjs\` resolves the CLI at runtime, first match wins:
+
+1. \`$SPECLANG_CLI\` — explicit override
+2. \`node_modules/speclang\` — a real install, e.g.
+   \`npm install --save-dev https://github.com/dexdat/SpecLang\`
+3. \`node_modules/.bin/speclang\` — a linked or globally installed CLI
+4. the CLI that generated this project: \`${generatorCli}\`
+
+Use a different CLI without touching the project:
+
+\`\`\`bash
+SPECLANG_CLI=/path/to/SpecLang/bin/speclang npm run validate
+\`\`\`
+
+Why not a dependency? The \`speclang\` package on npm is an unrelated project,
+so a \`file:\` or registry dependency here would either point at a path that
+exists on one machine only or install the wrong code. The shim keeps the
+project portable: point it at any SpecLang checkout or install.
+
+If the resolved CLI reports "SpecLang not built", that checkout needs its
+TypeScript build once (\`npm run build\` inside the SpecLang checkout itself).
+`;
